    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "The hypothesis concerns the relationship between constraint count (w) and the minimum network capacity (N_c(w)) required to achieve zero violations in a bootstrap solution space. I will design and execute a numerical experiment using a small feedforward neural network to probe this rigidity transition. The experiment will systematically vary the number of constraints (w) and measure the minimum hidden layer width (N_c) that achieves zero violation on held-out constraints. This will determine whether the solution space becomes more rigid (N_c decreases/stays constant) or fatter (N_c increases) as constraints are added. I'll implement the experiment in Python, run the grid search, and analyze the N_c(w) trend to validate or refute the hypothesis."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The experiment will produce a dataset of (w, N_c) pairs. If N_c(w) remains constant or decreases as w increases, it supports the rigidity hypothesis (Betti number drop). If N_c(w) increases with w, it supports the \"fat\" solution space hypothesis. I expect to observe a clear threshold behavior where N_c(w) changes abruptly at a critical w, consistent with a topological phase transition in the constraint manifold."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
